  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 201 AM CST Sat Nov 21 2026\n\nExpect today's weather to be a near repeat of Friday's as the \narea remains wedged between deep western CONUS troughing and \nnortheastern CONUS ridging. Near record high temperatures are in\nstore for the area with 925 hPa above the 95th percentile for \nlate November (around 15C observed on the 00Z ILX/DVN RAOBs). \nThough today's record high of 72F (73F) at Chicago (Rockford) \nmay be a bit out of reach, the forecast high near/at 70F for \nmuch of the area will be quite rare for this late in the season.\nThere have been only 4 occurrences (1 occurrence) on record of \n70F or higher at Chicago (Rockford) this late in the season. \nEven lowering the threshold to 68F (Friday's observed high for \nChicago), there have been only 13 (4) occurrences at Chicago \n(Rockford) this late. The very mild conditions will continue \ntonight with nighttime lows in the lower 50s.\n\nA mid-level wave currently centered near Las Vegas will become \nincreasingly sheared while crossing the Upper Mississippi River \nValley on Sunday. The alignment of a surface cold front, sheared\nmid-level wave, departing right entrance of a modest upper- \nlevel jet streak, and PWAT values up to 1.2 inches (above 90th \npercentile) will support an axis of rain drifting eastward \nacross the CWA through the day. Though mid-level lapse rates \nwill be marginal at best, would not be surprised to see sporadic\nlightning as close as west-central Illinois. Blustery northwest\nwinds will accompany the passage of the cold front.\n\nA substantially colder and drier airmass will build over the \narea Monday in the wake of Sunday's cold front. Temperatures \nwill be seasonably cold for late November, with some areas \nacross interior northern Illinois possibly seeing lows in the \nteens Monday night. Though low-level flow will turn \nnorth/northeast Monday night through Tuesday, marginal SST-850 \nhPa delta temps and a strong inversion around 6kft should limit \nlake effect precip to only some flurries or very light snow \nshowers. \n\nGuidance continues to struggle with the evolution of a complex \nmid- level pattern Wednesday through Thanksgiving as a wave \ndigging southeast across the Upper Mississippi Valley interacts \nwith a Gulf low lifting northeast toward the Mid-Atlantic. There\nremains a small, but non-negligable, number of ensemble members\nas well as the deterministic GFS suggesting that the two \nsystems will phase into a larger trough over the Ohio Valley. If\nthis scenario occurs, mixed precip would be possible across at \nleast the southeastern CWA on Thursday, though precip will have \nto contend with the antecedent very dry airmass.\n\nKluber",
